Background:
The idea is simple .. provide Orion specific extensions in the ARM64 arch layer to do 
read and write pte operations to maintain page table coherence.

We ve already covered the write part in previous meetings (set_pte_at) this one talks about
the read part viz., pte_offset_map(pmd_t*, vaddr) Theres a pte_offset_map_lock that 
also takes the lock.

The above takes a last level page pointer, breaks up address to get pte_offset and indexes
into the above page table (std va => page lookup tricks)

Shadow Page Table now refers to a hash table that is keyed by cpu pte PA and valued 
with shadow gpu pte PA

Drawbacks:
-> we will have to #ifdef ORION  pgtable.h which is arch specific. It appears that might
   have to happen anyways so probably not a big deal

-> hash table has a space price. On the brighter side we only store shadowed cpu ptes
   not all cpu ptes





Details:
arch/arm/include/asm/pgtable.h
static inline pte_t* pte_offset_map(pmd_t* ptable, vaddr) {
       pte_pa = use std arm64 implementation to go from page table base to pte offset
       pte_t *ptep = kmap_atomic(pte_pa); // this is how ARM32 works

// ++++ new code 
#ifdef ORION
       if (pte_p has shadow bit set) {
	  gpu_pte* gp_pte = hash_get(shadow_pagetable, pte_pa);
	  update cpu ptes with gpu pte ACCESS/DIRTY/OTHER status
       }
#else
	return pte_p;
}


RACE Conditions and window of disagreement:
long story short when OS asks for a pte pointer we sync gpu/cpu LRU status
    while that decreases the window it doesnt make it zero even when OS takes
    the PTE lock.

    typical OS usage:
    pte_t* ptep = pte_offset_map(ppgtable, vaddr);
    
    pte_t localpte = *ptep;
    if (PTE_DIRTY(localpte) { do something }

between offset_map and pointer dereference GPU can write to the page and dirty it.

But hold on the same can happen for the CPU PTE. Nothing stops another core from writing
to that page. 

To do this safely at times where it makes a functional difference OS has to write protect
the page. This is discussed in details in a memory corruption bug Linus and gang were 
chasing down .. 
http://kerneltrap.org/node/7517
<follow the conversation between Linus and David Miller>

Heres the code that does so and it does use write protect on page:
clear_page_dirty_for_io (page-writeback.c)	
           if (page_mkclean(page)) // write protects first then checks
                        set_page_dirty(page);




